## Java 8 — the foundational modern Java release

### 1. Lambdas and functional interfaces

Before Java 8, callbacks needed anonymous classes.

```java
// Java 7 style
Runnable oldStyle = new Runnable() {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Running");
    }
};

// Java 8+
Runnable modern = () -> System.out.println("Running");
```

A lambda works where Java expects a **functional interface**: an interface with one abstract method, such as `Runnable`, `Comparator<T>`, `Function<T, R>`, `Predicate<T>`, or `Consumer<T>`.

```java
List<String> names = List.of("Asha", "Ravi", "Mina");

names.sort((a, b) -> a.compareToIgnoreCase(b));
names.forEach(System.out::println);
```

### 2. Streams

Streams express a pipeline: transform, filter, group, aggregate. They do not store data; they process a source.

```java
List<String> names = List.of("asha", "ravi", "mina", "alex");

List<String> result = names.stream()
    .filter(name -> name.length() >= 4)
    .map(String::toUpperCase)
    .sorted()
    .toList(); // Java 16+. Use collect(toList()) on Java 8.

System.out.println(result); // [ALEX, ASHA, MINA, RAVI]
```

A common business example:

```java
record Order(String customer, double total) {}

List<Order> orders = List.of(
    new Order("Asha", 1200),
    new Order("Ravi", 800),
    new Order("Asha", 500)
);

double ashaSpend = orders.stream()
    .filter(order -> order.customer().equals("Asha"))
    .mapToDouble(Order::total)
    .sum();

System.out.println(ashaSpend); // 1700.0
```

Use streams for readable transformations. Avoid forcing them into complex stateful logic; an ordinary loop is often clearer there.

### 3. `Optional`

`Optional<T>` makes absence explicit at an API boundary.

```java
Optional<String> findEmail(long userId) {
    return userId == 1
        ? Optional.of("asha@example.com")
        : Optional.empty();
}

String email = findEmail(2)
    .orElse("unknown@example.com");
```

Prefer:

```java
userRepository.findById(id)
    .map(User::email)
    .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown user"));
```

Avoid using `Optional` for fields, constructor parameters, or database entities in most application code. It is primarily a return-type tool.

### 4. `java.time`

Replace legacy mutable date/time APIs (`Date`, `Calendar`) with immutable, timezone-aware types.

```java
import java.time.*;

Instant now = Instant.now();                         // an absolute point in time
LocalDate invoiceDate = LocalDate.of(2026, 9, 6);    // no time zone
ZonedDateTime indiaNow = ZonedDateTime.now(ZoneId.of("Asia/Kolkata"));

LocalDate dueDate = invoiceDate.plusDays(30);
System.out.println(dueDate); // 2026-10-06
```

Use:

- `Instant` for database timestamps and event time.
- `LocalDate` for birthdays, billing dates, and date-only concepts.
- `ZonedDateTime` when the actual geographic time zone matters.
- `Duration` / `Period` for elapsed time or calendar periods.

### 5. `CompletableFuture`

This enables asynchronous composition without manually managing callbacks.

```java
CompletableFuture<String> userFuture =
    CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> "Asha");

CompletableFuture<String> greetingFuture = userFuture
    .thenApply(name -> "Hello, " + name)
    .exceptionally(error -> "Hello, guest");

System.out.println(greetingFuture.join());
```

## Java 9 — modules and platform modernization

### 1. Java Platform Module System (JPMS)

Modules let an application declare dependencies and control which packages it exposes.

```java
// src/com.example.app/module-info.java
module com.example.app {
    requires java.net.http;
    exports com.example.app.api;
}
```

Benefits:

- More reliable dependency boundaries.
- Better encapsulation.
- Custom small runtimes with `jlink`.

Caution: modularizing an old application can be a real migration project due to reflection, split packages, and dependencies that are not modular-aware. You can run most classpath applications on modern Java without creating `module-info.java`.

### 2. Collection factory methods

```java
List<String> roles = List.of("ADMIN", "USER");
Set<Integer> ids = Set.of(10, 20, 30);
Map<String, Integer> scores = Map.of("Asha", 95, "Ravi", 88);
```

These collections are immutable and reject `null`.

### 3. JShell and improved process handling

```java
ProcessHandle.current()
    .info()
    .command()
    .ifPresent(System.out::println);
```

Java 9 also introduced `Flow`, the standard reactive-streams-style interfaces.

## Java 10 — `var`

`var` gives local-variable type inference. It does not create dynamic typing: the compiler still determines one fixed static type.

```java
var users = new ArrayList<String>();
var total = 42;
var request = HttpRequest.newBuilder();
```

Good:

```java
var customerById = new HashMap<Long, Customer>();
```

Less good, because the meaning is unclear:

```java
var x = compute();
```

Rules:

- Only local variables.
- Must have an initializer.
- Cannot be `null` alone.
- Cannot be fields, method parameters, or return types.

```java
var name = null; // does not compile
```

## Java 11 — a major practical baseline

### 1. Standard HTTP Client

This replaced the old, awkward `HttpURLConnection` for most use cases.

```java
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.http.*;

HttpClient client = HttpClient.newHttpClient();

HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
    .uri(URI.create("https://api.example.com/users/1"))
    .header("Accept", "application/json")
    .GET()
    .build();

HttpResponse<String> response = client.send(
    request,
    HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString()
);

System.out.println(response.statusCode());
System.out.println(response.body());
```

Async version:

```java
client.sendAsync(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString())
    .thenApply(HttpResponse::body)
    .thenAccept(System.out::println)
    .join();
```

### 2. Better String and file APIs

```java
String raw = "  hello\nworld  ";

System.out.println(raw.isBlank());     // false
System.out.println(raw.strip());       // removes Unicode whitespace
System.out.println("ha".repeat(3));    // hahaha

raw.lines().forEach(System.out::println);

String content = Files.readString(Path.of("config.json"));
Files.writeString(Path.of("output.txt"), "saved");
```

### 3. Launch a single source file directly

```bash
java Hello.java
```

This is useful for scripts, demos, coding exercises, and small operational utilities.

## Java 12–15 — cleaner control flow and multiline 
### Switch expressions (final in Java 14)

Old `switch` statements were error-prone because `break` was optional.

```java
String label;

switch (status) {
    case 200:
        label = "OK";
        break;
    case 404:
        label = "Not Found";
        break;
    default:
        label = "Other";
}
```

Modern switch expressions return a value and avoid accidental fall-through.

```java
int status = 404;

String label = switch (status) {
    case 200 -> "OK";
    case 404 -> "Not Found";
    case 401, 403 -> "Unauthorized";
    default -> "Other";
};
```

For multiple statements, use `yield`.

```java
String grade = switch (score) {
    case 90, 91, 92, 93, 94, 95, 96, 97, 98, 99, 100 -> "A";
    default -> {
        logScore(score);
        yield "Other";
    }
};
```

### Text blocks (final in Java 15)

Text blocks make JSON, SQL, HTML, and multiline messages readable.

```java
String json = """
    {
      "name": "Asha",
      "role": "ADMIN"
    }
    """;

String sql = """
    SELECT id, name
    FROM users
    WHERE active = true
    ORDER BY name
    """;
```

## Java 14–17 — model data and type hierarchies clearly

### Records (final in Java 16)

Records are concise immutable data carriers. The compiler generates the constructor, accessors, `equals`, `hashCode`, and `toString`.

```java
public record User(long id, String name, String email) {}
```

Usage:

```java
User user = new User(1, "Asha", "asha@example.com");

System.out.println(user.name());
System.out.println(user);
```

Records can validate input in their compact constructor.

```java
public record Money(String currency, long cents) {
    public Money {
        if (cents < 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Amount cannot be negative");
        }
    }
}
```

Use records for DTOs, API payloads, value objects, commands, events, and immutable configuration. Do not use them automatically for JPA entities or objects whose identity/mutable lifecycle matters.

### Pattern matching for `instanceof` (final in Java 16)

```java
Object value = "Java";

if (value instanceof String text) {
    System.out.println(text.toUpperCase());
}
```

This replaces repetitive casting:

```java
if (value instanceof String) {
    String text = (String) value;
}
```

### Sealed classes (final in Java 17)

Sealed classes limit which types may extend a hierarchy.

```java
public sealed interface PaymentResult
    permits PaymentSuccess, PaymentFailure {}

public record PaymentSuccess(String transactionId)
    implements PaymentResult {}

public record PaymentFailure(String reason)
    implements PaymentResult {}
```

This is excellent for finite business domains: payment outcomes, AST nodes, UI states, workflow commands, or API result types.

```java
String message = switch (result) {
    case PaymentSuccess success -> "Paid: " + success.transactionId();
    case PaymentFailure failure -> "Failed: " + failure.reason();
};
```

The compiler can prove the switch is exhaustive because it knows every permitted subtype.

### Helpful NullPointerExceptions

Modern Java can identify the null part of a chained access more precisely.

```java
String city = order.customer().address().city();
```

Instead of only “NullPointerException,” Java can report which expression was null. This is diagnostic help, not a substitute for validation and sound null-handling design.

## Java 18–20 — previews that led to modern concurrency

### Java 18

Important smaller developer-facing improvements:

- UTF-8 became the default charset.
- A simple development HTTP file server appeared:

```bash
jwebserver -p 8080
```

### Virtual threads: preview in 19/20, final in Java 21

Traditional platform threads map closely to OS threads, so blocking I/O at very high concurrency can be expensive. Virtual threads are lightweight JVM-managed threads designed for massive numbers of mostly-blocking tasks.

```java
try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    List<Callable<String>> tasks = List.of(
        () -> fetchCustomer(1),
        () -> fetchCustomer(2),
        () -> fetchCustomer(3)
    );

    List<Future<String>> results = executor.invokeAll(tasks);

    for (Future<String> result : results) {
        System.out.println(result.get());
    }
}
```

For request-per-task server workloads, code can stay simple and blocking:

```java
Thread.startVirtualThread(() -> {
    String response = callRemoteService();
    saveResponse(response);
});
```

Virtual threads are not a magic “make everything parallel” switch.

Use them when work spends significant time waiting on I/O:

- HTTP calls
- database calls
- queues
- file/network I/O

They do not speed up CPU-bound workloads. For CPU-heavy work, bound concurrency around available processors.

## Java 21 LTS — the biggest upgrade since Java 8

### 1. Virtual threads

Finalized in Java 21. This is the strongest reason many server applications moved to Java 21. The official JEP describes them as lightweight threads intended to simplify high-throughput concurrent applications. [JEP 444](https://openjdk.org/jeps/444)

```java
try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    for (int userId = 1; userId <= 100_000; userId++) {
        int id = userId;
        executor.submit(() -> sendNotification(id));
    }
}
```

Do not pool virtual threads. A virtual-thread-per-task executor is normally the intended model.

### 2. Pattern matching for `switch`

This works especially well with records and sealed types.

```java
sealed interface Shape permits Circle, Rectangle {}
record Circle(double radius) implements Shape {}
record Rectangle(double width, double height) implements Shape {}

double area(Shape shape) {
    return switch (shape) {
        case Circle(double radius) ->
            Math.PI * radius * radius;
        case Rectangle(double width, double height) ->
            width * height;
    };
}
```

This replaces a long sequence of `instanceof` checks and casts, while retaining compile-time exhaustiveness.

### 3. Record patterns

Destructure nested records directly.

```java
record Address(String city, String country) {}
record Person(String name, Address address) {}

void printCountry(Person person) {
    if (person instanceof Person(String name,
                                 Address(String city, String country))) {
        System.out.println(name + " lives in " + country);
    }
}
```

### 4. Sequenced collections

Java 21 standardized APIs for collections with an encounter order.

```java
List<String> queue = new ArrayList<>(List.of("first", "second", "third"));

System.out.println(queue.getFirst());  // first
System.out.println(queue.getLast());   // third
System.out.println(queue.reversed());  // [third, second, first]
```

This removes common awkwardness around `list.get(0)` and `list.get(list.size() - 1)`.

## Java 22–24 — native interop, pipelines, tooling, security

### Foreign Function & Memory API (final in Java 22)

This is a safer, supported replacement path for much JNI usage. It lets Java call native libraries and manage off-heap memory using standard APIs.

Conceptually:

```java
// Illustrative outline; exact native-library setup is environment-specific.
try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined()) {
    MemorySegment buffer = arena.allocate(1024);
    buffer.set(ValueLayout.JAVA_INT, 0, 42);

    int value = buffer.get(ValueLayout.JAVA_INT, 0);
    System.out.println(value);
}
```

Use it for performance-sensitive native integration, OS APIs, C libraries, hardware SDKs, or ML/graphics libraries. It is more advanced than ordinary application programming; do not reach for it merely to read files or make HTTP calls.

### Unnamed variables and patterns (Java 22)

Use `_` for values intentionally ignored.

```java
try {
    process();
} catch (Exception _) {
    // Exception object intentionally unused
}
```

```java
record Point(int x, int y) {}

if (point instanceof Point(int x, _)) {
    System.out.println("x = " + x);
}
```

### Stream Gatherers (final in Java 24)

Gatherers allow custom intermediate stream operations. A practical built-in example is windowing.

```java
import java.util.stream.Gatherers;

List<List<Integer>> windows = List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 5).stream()
    .gather(Gatherers.windowFixed(2))
    .toList();

System.out.println(windows); // [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5]]
```

### Class-File API (final in Java 24)

This supplies a standard Java API to parse, generate, and transform `.class` files, useful for framework authors, bytecode tooling, agents, and code generators. It is not something typical application code needs.

### Markdown in Javadoc (Java 23)

```java
/// Returns the active user.
///
/// ## Errors
///
/// Throws `IllegalArgumentException` when `id` is invalid.
User findActiveUser(long id) {
    // ...
}
```

## Java 25 LTS — current long-term-support target

Java 25 is the newest LTS release. Its major production-ready additions include:

- **Scoped Values** — an efficient, safer way to pass immutable contextual data down a call chain.
- **Module import declarations** — import all public packages exported by a module.
- **Compact source files and instance `main` methods** — simpler beginner/scripting source files.
- **Flexible constructor bodies** — statements can appear before an explicit `super(...)` call in controlled cases.
- **KDF API** — standard cryptographic key-derivation functions.
- **Generational Shenandoah** and performance/observability improvements.

Java 25 has eighteen JEPs, including finalization of Scoped Values and module imports. [OpenJDK’s JDK 25 GA announcement](https://mail.openjdk.org/archives/list/jdk-dev@openjdk.org/latest?count=100&page=2)

### Scoped Values

Instead of threading a value through every method parameter, bind it for one dynamic scope.

```java
static final ScopedValue<String> REQUEST_ID = ScopedValue.newInstance();

void handleRequest(String requestId) {
    ScopedValue.where(REQUEST_ID, requestId)
        .run(() -> {
            log("Request started");
            callService();
        });
}

void log(String message) {
    System.out.println("[" + REQUEST_ID.get() + "] " + message);
}
```

Use this for request IDs, trace context, security context, or tenant context—especially with virtual threads. Do not use it as a hidden replacement for ordinary business-data parameters.

### Module imports

```java
import module java.base;
```

This imports accessible public types exported by the module. It is useful for compact programs, but explicit imports often remain clearer in large applications because they show dependencies directly.

## Java 26 — latest feature release

Java 26 contains ten JEPs. The significant production-facing ones are:

### HTTP/3 for `HttpClient`

Java’s standard HTTP client can use HTTP/3, which runs over QUIC. Benefits can include reduced head-of-line blocking and faster connection establishment in appropriate network conditions.

```java
HttpClient client = HttpClient.newBuilder()
    .version(HttpClient.Version.HTTP_3)
    .build();

HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder(
        URI.create("https://example.com/api/status"))
    .GET()
    .build();

HttpResponse<String> response = client.send(
    request,
    HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString()
);
```

The server/network must support HTTP/3; client preference does not guarantee HTTP/3 will be used.

### Prepare to Make `final` Mean Final

Java 26 strengthens the direction toward preventing deep reflection from silently mutating `final` fields. This improves assumptions made by developers, frameworks, and JVM optimizations.

```java
public final class Config {
    private final String endpoint;

    public Config(String endpoint) {
        this.endpoint = endpoint;
    }

    public String endpoint() {
        return endpoint;
    }
}
```

Frameworks relying on reflective mutation should migrate toward constructors, factories, supported serialization hooks, or explicitly authorized mechanisms.

### Ahead-of-Time object caching

JDK 26 adds AOT object caching capabilities to improve startup behavior by reusing pre-initialized object graphs. This matters more for startup-sensitive workloads—CLI programs, serverless functions, and large frameworks—than for ordinary code structure.

### G1 GC synchronization improvements

G1 reduces unnecessary synchronization to improve throughput. This is a runtime improvement: most applications benefit without source changes.

### Preview/incubator features in Java 26

Java 26 also continues several experimental APIs/features:

- Structured Concurrency, sixth preview
- Primitive types in patterns / `instanceof` / `switch`, fourth preview
- PEM cryptographic encodings, second preview
- Lazy constants, second preview
- Vector API, eleventh incubator

Use preview features only deliberately:

```bash
javac --enable-preview --release 26 Example.java
java --enable-preview Example
```

Preview code must run on the same feature-release JDK and can change or disappear. The Java language specification explicitly treats preview features as release-specific. [Java SE 26 language updates](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/26/language/java-se-language-updates.pdf)

## What to use in real projects

If starting a production service today:

- Choose **Java 25 LTS** when long support life matters most.
- Choose **Java 21 LTS** when dependency compatibility or organizational adoption makes it the safer immediate jump.
- Use **Java 26** for experimenting with its final features or when your deployment model supports frequent upgrades.
- Use Java 8 only when constrained by legacy dependencies; it is far behind in language ergonomics, observability, concurrency, and platform support.

The modern Java core toolkit is:

```java
// Data
record User(long id, String name) {}

// Finite domain
sealed interface Result permits Success, Failure {}
record Success(User user) implements Result {}
record Failure(String message) implements Result {}

// Exhaustive branching
String display(Result result) {
    return switch (result) {
        case Success(User user) -> "Welcome " + user.name();
        case Failure(String message) -> "Error: " + message;
    };
}

// High-concurrency blocking work
void notifyUsers(List<User> users) throws Exception {
    try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
        for (User user : users) {
            executor.submit(() -> sendEmail(user));
        }
    }
}
```

That combination—**records, sealed hierarchies, pattern-matching switch, standard HTTP client, `java.time`, streams where appropriate, and virtual threads**—is the most important conceptual shift from Java 8 to modern Java.